<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klaipėda Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Memel Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> Convention was an international agreement signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in May 1924 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It recognised Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the Klaipėda Region, taken by Lithuania in the revolt of 1923, while granting the region wide autonomy with its own elected parliament and directorate. The port was to serve the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> basin, and the arrangement lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> took the region in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention.jpg" alt="Klaipėda Convention" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memelland_1923-1939.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The lands north of the Neman River had belonged to a German state since their conquest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-crusade">Prussian Crusade</a> of the 13th century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the region was detached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and placed, from 10 January 1920, under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>, with the French as temporary administrators. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> claimed it for its Lithuanian-speaking Prussian Lithuanians and because the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s only viable access to the sea, but the claim found little support, and a free city like the Free City of Danzig seemed likely. The Lithuanian government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-riflemens-union">Lithuanian Riflemen's Union</a> therefore organised a revolt in January 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 16 February 1923 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors">Conference of Ambassadors</a> transferred the district to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on condition that a treaty be signed later. A commission under the French diplomat Jules Laroche proposed a draft giving the Second Polish Republic extensive rights in the port, which Lithuania, locked in a bitter dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> over the Vilnius Region, rejected; its delegation under Ernestas Galvanauskas offered its own draft. With the talks deadlocked, the matter went to the League, whose commission under the American diplomat Norman Davis presented a draft on 18 February 1924. The League adopted the convention on 14 March, despite Polish protests, and it took full effect on 25 August 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The convention had 18 articles. Residents became Lithuanian citizens but had 18 months to opt for German citizenship, and were exempt from military service until January 1930. Lithuanian and German had equal status as official languages. The governor, appointed by the President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, could veto local laws only if they broke the statute, the Constitution of Lithuania or international agreements, and the region could not be transferred without the consent of the contracting parties. Any member of the League Council could raise breaches, a provision Nazi Germany later used to accuse Lithuania of violating minority rights.</p><p>The port was declared of international concern and run by a three-member Harbor Board, one member appointed by the Lithuanian government, one by the Directorate and one by the League. A transit agreement guaranteed freedom of transit, above all for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> hailed the convention as their major diplomatic victory, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> received no special rights in the port.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Klaipėda Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Klaipėda Convention</b> of 1924, the region was an autonomous part of the Republic of Lithuania, with its own parliament and directorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/klaipeda-region">Klaipėda Region</a></li></ul></section>
